  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Pronouns and the verb 'to be'",
      "body": "The present tense of **હોવું** 'to be' changes with the subject:\n\nહું **છું** *huṃ chhuṃ* — I am\nતું **છે** *tuṃ chhe* — you are (intimate)\nએ / તે **છે** *e / te chhe* — he/she/it is\nઅમે / આપણે **છીએ** *ame / āpaṇe chhīe* — we are\nતમે / આપ **છો** *tame / āp chho* — you are (polite/plural)\nએ લોકો / તેઓ **છે** *e loko / teo chhe* — they are\n\nThe negative is a single word for everyone: **નથી** *nathī* 'am not / is not / are not'. Note the two words for 'we': **અમે** excludes the listener, **આપણે** includes them ('you and I').",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "હું વિદ્યાર્થી છું.", "roman": "huṃ vidyārthī chhuṃ.", "en": "I am a student." },
        { "native": "એ મારો મિત્ર છે.", "roman": "e māro mitra chhe.", "en": "He is my friend." },
        { "native": "અમે અમેરિકાથી છીએ.", "roman": "ame Amerikāthī chhīe.", "en": "We (not you) are from America." },
        { "native": "આપણે ગુજરાતી છીએ.", "roman": "āpaṇe gujarātī chhīe.", "en": "We (you and I) are Gujarati." },
        { "native": "તમે ડૉક્ટર છો?", "roman": "tame ḍokṭar chho?", "en": "Are you a doctor?" },
        { "native": "ના, હું ડૉક્ટર નથી.", "roman": "nā, huṃ ḍokṭar nathī.", "en": "No, I'm not a doctor." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Question words go before the verb",
      "body": "Question words — **શું** 'what', **કોણ** 'who', **ક્યાં** 'where', **ક્યાંથી** 'from where', **કેમ** 'why/how' — don't move to the front as in English. They sit where the answer would go, usually just before the verb.\n\nFor yes/no questions, keep the statement order and raise your voice at the end. You may put **શું** at the very beginning to mark a yes/no question, especially in writing.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "તમારું નામ શું છે?", "roman": "tamāruṃ nām shuṃ chhe?", "en": "What is your name?" },
        { "native": "એ કોણ છે?", "roman": "e koṇ chhe?", "en": "Who is he/she?" },
        { "native": "તમે ક્યાં રહો છો?", "roman": "tame kyāṃ raho chho?", "en": "Where do you live?" },
        { "native": "તમે કેમ હસો છો?", "roman": "tame kem haso chho?", "en": "Why are you laughing?" },
        { "native": "શું તમે ગુજરાતી છો?", "roman": "shuṃ tame gujarātī chho?", "en": "Are you Gujarati?" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "'I know Gujarati': મને … આવડે છે",
      "body": "Gujarati often expresses knowing a skill with a **dative subject**: the person who knows is marked with -ને (મને 'to me', તમને 'to you'), and the language or skill is the grammatical subject of **આવડવું**. Literally: 'To me Gujarati comes'. You'll see more dative-subject verbs in Unit 5 (ગમવું 'to like').\n\nમને *mane* 'to me', તને *tane* 'to you (intimate)', તમને *tamne* 'to you (polite)', એને *ene* 'to him/her'.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "મને અંગ્રેજી આવડે છે.", "roman": "mane aṅgrejī āvaḍe chhe.", "en": "I know English." },
        { "native": "તમને હિન્દી આવડે છે?", "roman": "tamne hindī āvaḍe chhe?", "en": "Do you know Hindi?" },
        { "native": "એને ગુજરાતી નથી આવડતું.", "roman": "ene gujarātī nathī āvaḍtuṃ.", "en": "He/She doesn't know Gujarati." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "dialogue": {
    "title": "A new classmate",
    "setting": "Sam, a British student, meets Nirav at a Gujarati class in Vadodara.",
