      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "મારું નામ સેમ છે. તમારું નામ?", "roman": "māruṃ nām Sem chhe. tamāruṃ nām?", "en": "My name is Sam. And yours?" },
      { "speaker": "Nirav", "native": "હું નીરવ. તમે ક્યાંથી છો?", "roman": "huṃ Nīrav. tame kyāṃthī chho?", "en": "I'm Nirav. Where are you from?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "હું ઇંગ્લેન્ડથી છું, પણ અત્યારે વડોદરામાં રહું છું.", "roman": "huṃ Iṅglenḍthī chhuṃ, paṇ atyāre Vaḍodarāmāṃ rahuṃ chhuṃ.", "en": "I'm from England, but right now I live in Vadodara." },
      { "speaker": "Nirav", "native": "વાહ! તમને ગુજરાતી આવડે છે?", "roman": "vāh! tamne gujarātī āvaḍe chhe?", "en": "Wow! Do you know Gujarati?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "થોડું થોડું. હું શીખું છું.", "roman": "thoḍuṃ thoḍuṃ. huṃ shīkhuṃ chhuṃ.", "en": "A little. I'm learning." },
      { "speaker": "Nirav", "native": "બહુ સરસ! અમે બધા તમને મદદ કરીશું.", "roman": "bahu saras! ame badhā tamne madad karīshuṃ.", "en": "Very nice! We'll all help you." },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "આભાર! તમને મળીને આનંદ થયો.", "roman": "ābhār! tamne maḷīne ānand thayo.", "en": "Thanks! Nice to meet you." }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "On meeting someone, Gujaratis quickly ask where you are from — and often which town or community your family comes from. Answering with a city (*Amdāvād*, *Surat*, *Rājkoṭ*) is perfectly fine. Gujarati speakers switch freely into English and Hindi words; don't be surprised if your Gujarati question gets a mixed answer.",
